<DocPage
  tree={vipyrTree(Astro.url.pathname)}
  title="Ports · vipyr · Viv"
  icon="/brand/vipyr/vipyr-icon.svg"
  image="/brand/vipyr/vipyr-icon-512.png"
  description="Ports of upstream scientific codes to Viv, each a vipyr distribution under its upstream's license."
>
  <h1>Ports</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A port is an upstream program rewritten in Viv and checked against the upstream code. Each one is a vipyr
    distribution, <code>vipyr-&lt;name&gt;</code>, under the upstream's license.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>vipyr-liquids</code>, the examples of Allen and Tildesley's <em>Computer Simulation of Liquids</em>
      (CC0-1.0), as <a href="/vipyr/liquids"><code>vp.liquids</code></a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>vipyr-dftatom</code>, dftatom by Ondřej Čertík, Jiří Vackář and John Pask (MIT), as
      <code>vp.dftatom</code>; the gallery's <a href="/vipyr/gallery/plot-atoms-against-nist">atoms against NIST</a>
      uses it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Neither is on PyPI yet; both run from a <a href="/vipyr/install">checkout</a>. Each carries the upstream license
    verbatim, a list of authors with the upstream authors first, a citation file, and a record of the upstream
    commit ported and every difference from it.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/vipyr/api">The API reference →</a></p>
</DocPage>
